    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The process of associating meaning with a visual symbol, such as a number or a letter, is established through learning that involves interactions of specific regions of the brain. In this study a visual learning task was created through which subjects learned to first categorize previously unknown symbols as letters or numbers (categorical learning) and then associate each symbol with a specific letter or number (translation learning). The protocol was tested in three consecutive stages: 1) behavioral analysis in a healthy control group (n=21), 2) behavioral analysis in an epileptic nonsurgical group (n=5), 3) and a combined behavioral and electrophysiological study in an epileptic surgical group implanted with intracranial recording electrodes (n=3). Efficacy of the protocol was proven with behavioral data collected among the three groups that tested response time to stimuli, accuracy of response, and confidence interval. Groups all had similar upward learning trends towards 80% accuracy in categorical learning phase before moving to the translation learning phase. This study validated that the protocol could be completed within a one-hour time frame. Intracranial electrophysiological recordings were also used to capture the spatial and temporal dynamics of brain activity during the testing protocol. As indicated by the power of high-gamma activity recorded from individual electrodes over the posterior superior temporal region, there was a medial to lateral build-up of neural activity over the course of the testing phases of the protocol. Our findings validated the feasibility of a novel experimental paradigm for studying the human brain during learning of new symbols. Future empirical knowledge obtained with this paradigm will provide valuable mechanistic information about the interactions among different brain regions when human subjects learn new visual symbols.",
